* {{cog|es|environar}} {{qualifier|obsolete}}
{{rel-bottom}}

====Verb====
{{en-verb}} {{term-label|en|transitive}}

# To [[encircle]] or [[surround#Verb|surround]] (someone or something).
#: {{synonyms|en|q1=obsolete|belay}}
#* {{RQ:Shakespeare Titus Andronicus Q1|act=III|scene=i|page=51|passage=For novv I ſtand as one vpon a rocke, / '''Inuirond''' vvith a vvildernes of ſea, / VVho markes the vvaxing tide, grovv vvaue by vvaue, / Expecting euer when ſome enuious ſurge, / VVill in his briniſh bovvels ſvvallovv him.}}
#* {{RQ:Spenser Faerie Queene|book=III|canto=V|stanza=39|page=473|passage=Into that foreſt farre they thence him led, / VVhere vvas their dvvelling, in a pleaſant glade, / VVith mountaines rovvnd about '''enuironed''', / And mightie vvoodes, vvhich did the valley ſhade, {{...}}}}
#* {{RQ:Milton Poems 1673|sonnet=XII|page=56|passage=I did but prompt the age to quit their cloggs / By the knovvn rules of antient libertie, / When ſtrait a barbarous noiſe '''environs''' me / Of Ovvles and Cuckoes, Aſſes, Apes and Doggs.}}
#* {{RQ:Cowper Task|page=12|passage=[W]ent I forth, and found, till then unknovvn, / A cottage, vvhither oft vve ſince repair: / 'Tis perch'd upon the green-hill top, but cloſe / '''Inviron'd''' vvith a ring of branching elms / That overhang the thatch, itſelf unſeen, / Peeps at the vale belovv; {{...}}}}
#* {{RQ:Dickens Haunted House|chapter=The Mortals in the House|page=1|column=1|passage=Under none of the accredited ghostly circumstances, and '''environed''' by none of the conventional ghostly surroundings, did I first make acquaintance with the house which is the subject of this Christmas piece.}}
## {{lb|en|often|military}} To encircle or surround (someone or something) so as to [[attack#Verb|attack]] from all [[side#Noun|sides]]; to [[beset]].
##: {{synonyms|en|beleaguer|besiege}}
##* {{RQ:Shakespeare Henry 6-3|act=II|scene=i|page=152|column=2|passage='''Enuironed''' he vvas vvith many foes, / And ſtood againſt them, as the hope of Troy / Againſt the Greekes, that vvould haue entred Troy.}}
## {{lb|en|heraldry|chiefly|passive|obsolete}} To encircle or surround (a [[heraldic]] [[element#Noun|element]] such as a [[charge#Noun|charge]] or [[escutcheon]] ([[shield#Noun|shield]])).
##* {{quote-book|en|author=John W[oody] Papworth|authorlink=John Woody Papworth|entry=1 ANNULET betw. or within&nbsp;… and in chief&nbsp;…|editor=Alfred W. Morant|title=An Alphabetical Dictionary of Coats of Arms Belonging to Families in Great Britain and Ireland;{{nb...|Forming an Extensive Ordinary of British Armorials; upon an Entirely New Plan, in which the Arms are Systematically Subdivided throughout, and so Arranged in Alphabetical Order that the Names of Families whose Shields are Found on Buildings, Monuments, Seals, Paintings, Plate, etc., whether Mediæval or Modern, can be Readily Ascertained.}}|location=London|publisher=T. Richards,{{nb...|37, Great Queen Street.}}|year=1874|page=4|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=rjEGAAAAQAAJ&pg=PA4|column=2|oclc=460013398|passage=Az[ure], an annulet '''environing''' a barrulet, betw[een] two bars and in chief a cross patty fitchy or.}}
# To [[cover#Verb|cover]], [[enclose]], or [[envelop]] (someone or something).
#: {{synonyms|en|q1=obsolete|belay}}
#* {{RQ:Fairfax Godfrey of Bulloigne|book=15|stanza=33|page=273|passage=Farre off a hill and mountaine high they ſpide, / VVhoſe top the cloudes '''enuiron''', cloath and hide; {{...}}}}
#* {{RQ:Donne Poems|year=1639|poem=Elegy XIIII|page=95|passage=Since ſhe muſt goe, and I muſt mourne, come night / '''Environ''' me vvith darkneſſe, vvhilſt I vvrite: / Shadovv that hell unto me, vvhich alone / I am to ſuffer vvhen my ſoule is gone.}}
#* {{RQ:Smollett Ferdinand|volume=II|chapter=His Return to England, and Midnight Pilgrimage to Monimia's Tomb|page=218|passage=In vain I stretch these eyes, '''environed''' with darkness undistinguishing and void: no object meets my view; no sound salutes mine ear, except the noisy wind that whistles through these vaulted caves of death.}}
#* {{RQ:Charlotte Bronte Jane Eyre|volume=II|chapter=X|page=268|passage=On sleeping, I continued in dreams the idea of a dark and gusty night. {{...}} [T]otal obscurity '''environed''' me; rain pelted me; {{...}}}}
# ''Followed by'' '''[[from]]''': to [[hide#Verb|hide]] or [[shield#Verb|shield]] (someone or something).
# {{lb|en|chiefly|passive}} Of a [[person#Noun|person]]: to be [[position#Verb|positioned]] or [[station#Verb|stationed]] around (someone or something) to [[attend]] to or [[protect]] them.
# {{lb|en|figuratively}} Of a [[situation#Noun|situation]] or [[state of affairs]], especially [[danger#Noun|danger]] or [[trouble#Noun|trouble]]: to [[happen#Verb|happen]] to and [[affect#Verb|affect]] (someone or something).
#* {{RQ:Butler Hudibras|part=1|canto=III|page=59|passage=Ay me! what perils do '''environ''' / The Man that meddles with cold Iron!}}
#* {{RQ:Smollett Ferdinand|volume=II|chapter=Monimia's Honour is Protected by the Interposition of Heaven|page=100|passage=[S]he knew not one person to whose protection she could have recourse, from the inexpressible woes that '''environed''' her: {{...}}}}
#* {{RQ:Radcliffe Udolpho|volume=II|chapter=III|page=130|passage=Thus the party continued to converſe, and, as far as civility vvould permit, to torture each other by mutual boaſts, vvhile they reclined on ſofas in the portico, and vvere '''environed''' vvith delights both from nature and art, by vvhich any honeſt minds vvould have been tempered to benevolence, and happy imaginations vvould have been ſoothed into enchantment.}}
#* {{RQ:Burney Camilla|volume=III|chapter=Traits of Eccentricity|page=384|passage=[H]e never ceaſed to experience the fondeſt intereſt in her happineſs, nor the moſt tender compaſſion for the dangers vvith vvhich he ſavv her '''environed'''.}}
# {{lb|en|obsolete}}
## To [[amount#Verb|amount to]] or [[encompass]] (a [[space#Noun|space]]).
## To [[travel#Verb|travel]] [[completely]] around (a [[place#Noun|place]] or [[thing]]); to [[circumnavigate]].

